VERDICT: FAIL

P1 .skilled/hooks/session-relaunch/lib/killer.cjs:405 — owner-write failure cleanup can delete a contender’s mutex — After checking the inode and finding no owner file, cleanup removes the path recursively. A contender can replace the directory and write its owner between those checks and the removal. The EEXIST test does not cover this non-EEXIST cleanup path.

P1 .skilled/hooks/session-relaunch/lib/killer.cjs:374 — restoration can leave an active replacement hidden under `.stale-*` — If a third contender recreates the mutex path after the mismatched directory is renamed, restoration can fail with `ENOTEMPTY` or `EEXIST`. The displaced holder may continue while its mutex is outside the canonical path, where the stale sweep can later remove it. The Round 2 test covers restoration only while the path is empty.

P2 .skilled/hooks/session-relaunch/README.md:130 — the mutex ownership description omits the token — The implementation writes a PID and token and checks both during release; the README describes release using only the inode and owner as only the PID.

The supplied evidence confirms the inode-pinning test fails on the base (55/56) and passes after the fix (56/56). Both Round 2 tests pass in the saved 58/58 TAP run, and the 20-run evidence is green. `core.lockOwnerPid` reads the first field as the PID; no other code reads or writes the mutex owner. The token does not break that reader. Added comments contain no ephemeral labels.

My focused rerun exited before exercising the tests because the sandbox denied the harness’s temporary `mkdtemp` with `EPERM` at `killer.test.cjs:25`. No repository files changed; the worktree remains three commits ahead of `origin/main`. No operator action is needed.